Rendered images of a white Keychron M1 used by the Lighting page preview.

Provenance: an original 3D render made for this project. The mouse was
modelled from scratch (parametric body, panels, slots, light pipes) and
rendered in Blender (Cycles). No Keychron photos, renders, CAD files or
textures were used in the scene; public product photos were only looked at
for proportions. These files are part of Tailglow and are released under the
repository license (GPL-3.0-or-later).

Layers per view (hero = 3/4 view, 800x600; top = top view, 700x700):

  base_lights_off.png      RGBA, transparent background, every LED dark,
                           contact shadow in the alpha channel
  zone_<name>.png          8-bit gray: the light one zone adds (emitter,
                           bounce on the shell, pool on the desk, halo)
  zone_<name>_direct.png   8-bit gray: coverage of the emitter surface itself

The app combines them in linear light (see src/render.rs):
  out = srgb(surface * (1 - 0.85 * max(direct * on)) + sum(tint * brightness * zone))
plus a soft glow spill (two wide blurs of the zone light).

Regenerate from the full-size render output with
  scripts/prepare-render-assets.py RENDER_DIR
